  III. THE e-phi COMMA
  ----------------------------------------

  ln(phi) = 0.4812118251
  = 0.48121... ~ 1/2 (error 0.01879)
  So e^1 ~ phi^2: e = 2.718282, phi^2 = 2.618034
  e/phi^2 = 1.0382912675 (+3.8291%)

  e ~ phi^2 with 3.4% error. Not bad!

  Better: ln(phi) ~ 29/60 = 0.48333...
  e^29 / phi^60 = ... too large to compute directly.
  But (e/phi^2)^29 ~ ... not useful.

  The CLEAN near-miss: e ~ phi^2.
  In rapidity: rapidity_e(phi^2) = ln(phi^2)/2 = ln(phi) = 0.481212
  rapidity_e(e) = ln(e)/2 = 1/2 = 0.5
  The gap: 0.5 - 0.481212 = 0.018788

  e has rapidity 0.5 (in its own base: trivially ln(e)/2 = 1/2).
  phi^2 has rapidity ln(phi) = 0.4812.
  Their rapidity gap: 0.5 - 0.4812 = 0.0188.
  In octaves: 0.0542
  = 0.054 octaves ~ 1/18 of an octave ~ the Pythagorean comma!

  e-phi^2 comma in octaves: 0.0542
  Pythagorean comma in octaves: 0.0196
  Ratio: 2.7730

  The e-phi^2 comma is 2.8x the Pythagorean comma.
  Not identical, but the same ORDER of magnitude.


